package com.hpcai270

// Days 135–151: Lustre, Storage Scale, BeeGFS and parallel filesystem engineering.
val hpcDaysPart14 = listOf(
Day(135,"Lustre architecture deep dive","Client, MDS/MDT and OSS/OST","Lustre separates metadata from bulk data. Clients access metadata services for namespace operations and object storage servers/targets for file data.","FLOW\n\nApplication → Lustre client → MDS/MDT for metadata + OSS/OST for data → storage media.\n\nThis separation lets bulk I/O scale independently from metadata capacity.","KEY OBJECTS\n\nMDS, MDT, OSS, OST, client, LNet, filesystem namespace. Know what each component owns and what failure looks like.","PERFORMANCE\n\nMetadata rate and aggregate bandwidth are different dimensions. A system can deliver huge sequential bandwidth while struggling with millions of tiny-file operations.","LAB\n\nDraw a 4-client, 2-OSS, 8-OST architecture. Mark metadata traffic and bulk data traffic separately.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain why Lustre can outperform NFS for large parallel reads without simply saying ‘it is faster’. Discuss concurrency and distributed targets.","GOAL\n\nThink in Lustre components, paths and failure domains."),
Day(136,"Lustre namespaces and MDTs","Metadata at scale","Metadata servers manage namespace information such as filenames, directories, permissions and file-to-object mappings. Metadata performance can become the limiting factor for AI pipelines with huge file counts.","METADATA OPERATIONS\n\ncreate, open, stat, lookup, rename, unlink, chmod, directory traversal. Millions of these operations can overwhelm a metadata service even when disks have bandwidth left.","DESIGN\n\nSeparate metadata workload from bulk data workload. SSD/NVMe-backed metadata is often valuable when file operations dominate.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nIf `ls` or dataset startup is slow while sequential bandwidth is healthy, suspect metadata rather than OST throughput.","LAB\n\nCreate a synthetic small-file workload and a large-file sequential workload. Identify which metrics diverge.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat is a metadata storm? Give an AI example: thousands of workers opening the same dataset structure simultaneously.","GOAL\n\nTreat metadata as a first-class performance resource."),
Day(137,"Lustre striping","Turning one file into parallel I/O","Striping distributes file objects across multiple OSTs. The goal is to allow many targets to serve one logical file concurrently.","CONCEPTS\n\nstripe count, stripe size, starting OST. Larger stripe counts can increase parallelism but may increase coordination or consume more targets.","WORKLOAD FIT\n\nLarge sequential files can benefit from broad striping. Small files or metadata-heavy workloads may gain little and can create unnecessary target activity.","OPERATIONS\n\nDo not apply one stripe policy to every dataset. Tune from measured workload characteristics.","LAB\n\nDesign stripe policies for 1 MB, 1 GB and 10 TB files. Explain why the policies differ.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat happens if a 100 GB file is stored on one OST in a system with 32 OSTs? Discuss single-target bandwidth versus parallel target access.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand striping as workload placement, not a magic performance switch."),
Day(138,"Lustre LNet","The transport layer between clients and servers","LNet provides Lustre networking and supports high-performance transports. In modern AI systems the network is part of the storage architecture.","MODEL\n\nLustre client/server → LNet → network transport → NIC/HCA → fabric. Multiple interfaces and routing can create resilient/high-bandwidth paths.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nCheck interface health, routes, LNet configuration, peer connectivity, congestion and server-side symptoms before changing filesystem parameters.","AI CONNECTION\n\nWhen storage is connected through RDMA-capable fabric, network latency and congestion can directly affect application I/O completion.","LAB\n\nMap every Lustre path in a diagram: client NIC, switch/fabric, server NIC and storage target. Mark redundant routes.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy should storage engineers understand the network when troubleshooting Lustre? Because storage traffic is network traffic in a disaggregated architecture.","GOAL\n\nSee Lustre and its fabric as one system."),
Day(139,"Lustre client behavior","Caching, locking and concurrency","The client is not a dumb mount. It participates in caching, locking and request processing. Application access patterns influence client CPU, memory and network behavior.","CONSIDER\n\nMany clients, many threads, repeated opens, sequential streaming, random access, shared-file writes and lock contention.","SYMPTOM\n\nHigh client CPU with low storage bandwidth may indicate metadata, lock or small-I/O overhead rather than slow disks.","OPERATIONS\n\nCompare client-side and server-side metrics. A server can look healthy while one client is misconfigured or overloaded.","LAB\n\nDesign a test with 1, 4 and 16 clients. Record aggregate bandwidth and per-client fairness.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow can one poorly behaved client affect a shared parallel filesystem? Discuss metadata load, network queues, target contention and fairness.","GOAL\n\nTreat clients as active participants in the filesystem."),
Day(140,"Lustre quotas and project governance","Multi-user capacity control","Large clusters need quotas and project accounting to prevent uncontrolled growth and to attribute usage.","MODEL\n\nUser/group/project identity → quota accounting → usage threshold → alert/enforcement.\n\nCapacity governance must align with research/project ownership.","RISKS\n\nA full filesystem is a cluster-wide incident. Quota configuration errors can block legitimate workloads or allow unexpected growth.","OPERATIONS\n\nTrack inode usage as well as bytes. Millions of small files can exhaust inodes before capacity.","LAB\n\nCreate a policy for 10 research projects including byte and inode limits, warning thresholds and emergency exceptions.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy are inode quotas important for AI? A dataset may consume relatively little space but millions of files can create severe metadata pressure.","GOAL\n\nManage both capacity and namespace scale."),
Day(141,"Storage Scale architecture","GPFS evolved for distributed workloads","IBM Storage Scale provides a clustered filesystem and data services architecture. Learn the concepts rather than memorizing vendor-specific command syntax.","ARCHITECTURE\n\nMultiple nodes participate in filesystem access. Data and metadata are distributed across the cluster, with policy and file placement capabilities.","CONCEPTS\n\nNSD, filesystem, pools, failure groups, quorum/manager roles, policies and protocols. Exact implementation/version details must be checked against the deployed release.","PERFORMANCE\n\nScale performance depends on client count, network, storage media, filesystem layout and workload. IBM documents performance best practices because misuse can defeat the architecture.","LAB\n\nDraw a Storage Scale cluster with protocol nodes, compute clients and storage nodes. Identify control, metadata and data paths.","INTERVIEW\n\nCompare Storage Scale with Lustre at the architectural level: both provide parallel shared filesystems but differ in ecosystem, management model and feature set.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand GPFS/Storage Scale as a production HPC platform."),
Day(142,"Storage Scale failure groups","Designing around correlated failures","Distributed filesystems must understand where data replicas or fragments live. Failure groups represent correlated failure domains such as a node, enclosure or rack.","PRINCIPLE\n\nTwo copies in one failure domain are not equivalent to two copies across independent domains.","DESIGN\n\nMap storage devices to physical topology. Define policies that avoid losing all required copies/fragments when one rack, node or enclosure fails.","OPERATIONS\n\nWhen hardware is replaced, verify that the new placement preserves intended failure-domain rules.","LAB\n\nDesign a 3-rack system with 2 copies. Explain where replicas should and should not live.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat is the difference between redundancy and fault-domain diversity? Provide a concrete example.","GOAL\n\nThink beyond disk-level failure."),
Day(143,"Storage Scale ILM and policies","Move data according to value","Policy-based placement lets data move between storage pools based on age, type, access pattern or business rules.","EXAMPLE\n\nHot dataset → NVMe/flash pool → warm capacity pool → object/archive.\n\nThe policy engine turns lifecycle intent into placement actions.","AI USE\n\nKeep active training data close to compute; move completed experiments and older checkpoints to cheaper tiers.","RISK\n\nA bad policy can move hot data away from GPUs or trigger massive migration traffic. Policies require testing and monitoring.","LAB\n\nWrite a lifecycle policy for datasets used daily, weekly and annually. Estimate movement volume.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow would you prevent ILM from interfering with a training job? Discuss classification, exclusions, scheduling and observability.","GOAL\n\nConnect data lifecycle with performance engineering."),
Day(144,"BeeGFS architecture","Another parallel filesystem","BeeGFS is a parallel filesystem commonly seen in HPC environments. Learn it as a second implementation so you understand what is fundamental versus vendor-specific.","COMPONENTS\n\nManagement, metadata, storage and client roles. Data can be distributed across storage targets for parallel access.","WHY IT MATTERS\n\nA strong engineer can compare architectures instead of being tied to one filesystem vocabulary.","PERFORMANCE\n\nClient concurrency, storage targets, network fabric and workload layout determine throughput. Metadata and data paths remain distinct concerns.","LAB\n\nCreate a comparison table: Lustre vs Storage Scale vs BeeGFS across metadata, data distribution, networking, administration and common use cases.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhen would you evaluate a second parallel filesystem instead of standardizing on one? Include skills, ecosystem, workload fit and operational cost.","GOAL\n\nBuild technology-independent storage judgment."),
Day(145,"Parallel filesystem benchmarking","FIO versus IOR versus application tests","Tools answer different questions. FIO is excellent for block/device-style workloads; IOR is common for parallel filesystem throughput; application benchmarks prove end-to-end usefulness.","MODEL\n\nDevice benchmark → filesystem benchmark → application benchmark. Each adds another layer of reality.","IOR VARIABLES\n\ntransfer size, block size, clients, processes, collective/independent I/O, file-per-process/shared file and filesystem layout.","AI CONNECTION\n\nA synthetic 100 GB/s test does not prove a training pipeline can consume 100 GB/s. The application must be measured with realistic data access.","LAB\n\nWrite a benchmark plan with three stages and explicit success criteria.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can an IOR result be higher than your application throughput? Discuss caching, access pattern, CPU preprocessing and synchronization.","GOAL\n\nKnow which benchmark proves which claim."),
Day(146,"Metadata benchmarking","Millions of files change the problem","Metadata benchmarks stress namespace operations rather than bulk bandwidth. AI pipelines can generate enormous file counts through checkpoints, shards, logs and preprocessing.","OPERATIONS\n\ncreate/stat/open/close/unlink/rename and directory traversal are separate workloads.","DESIGN\n\nMeasure operations per second, latency, concurrency and scaling with clients. Watch metadata CPU, SSD latency and network traffic.","LAB\n\nDesign a metadata test that creates 1 million small files in controlled batches. Define cleanup and failure-safe procedures.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can ‘the storage has 500 GB/s’ be irrelevant to a metadata storm? Because the workload is operation/latency constrained, not bulk-bandwidth constrained.","GOAL\n\nRecognize workload class before selecting hardware."),
Day(147,"Parallel filesystem recovery","What happens when targets fail","A production filesystem must continue operating safely during failures and recover without overwhelming surviving resources.","FAILURE FLOW\n\nTarget failure → degraded state → service continuity if protection permits → rebuild/rebalance → verification → return to normal.","RISK\n\nRecovery competes with user I/O. Aggressive rebuild can increase application latency; slow rebuild prolongs exposure to additional failures.","OPERATIONS\n\nMonitor degraded targets, rebuild throughput, remaining protection, queue depth and application impact.","LAB\n\nWrite a recovery runbook with stop conditions: when to throttle rebuild, when to add capacity and when to declare a larger incident.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is recovery performance part of storage architecture? Because a system is most vulnerable after the first failure.","GOAL\n\nDesign for degraded mode, not just healthy mode."),
Day(148,"Parallel filesystem upgrades","Change management at scale","Upgrading a filesystem or client fleet requires compatibility planning, sequencing, rollback and validation.","PLAN\n\nVersion matrix → lab validation → maintenance window → rolling/client strategy → server upgrade → health checks → workload validation → rollback criteria.","AI/HPC\n\nA driver, OFED, kernel or filesystem client mismatch can break high-performance paths. Treat the software stack as one compatibility graph.","LAB\n\nBuild a change plan for upgrading Linux kernel + NIC driver + filesystem client. Identify dependencies and rollback points.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow do you upgrade 500 compute nodes without creating a 500-node outage? Discuss canaries, partitions, drain/cordon, automation and health validation.","GOAL\n\nScale change management as carefully as scale-out storage."),
Day(149,"Lustre versus Storage Scale","Architecture trade-offs","Technology selection must be workload-driven. Compare ecosystem, metadata model, data distribution, policy features, protocol access, administration, support and existing skills.","DECISION MATRIX\n\nBandwidth → metadata → protocols → data lifecycle → failure model → operations → vendor support → cost.","IMPORTANT\n\nDo not select by benchmark headline alone. A system that wins one synthetic test may lose for your actual workload.","REAL WORLD\n\nNVIDIA architectures include certified storage partners and separate high-performance/user storage roles; the filesystem is one component of a broader platform.","LAB\n\nScore Lustre and Storage Scale for three workloads: LLM training, HPC simulation and general research files.","INTERVIEW\n\nDefend a technology choice while explicitly stating assumptions and what evidence could change your decision.","GOAL\n\nDemonstrate architecture judgment."),
Day(150,"Parallel filesystem security","Permissions, identity and isolation","HPC storage must preserve enterprise access control while serving thousands of clients efficiently.","LAYERS\n\nLinux identity → POSIX mode/ACL → filesystem policy → network trust → management access.\n\nNever assume the filesystem replaces OS security.","SHARED DATA\n\nProject groups, service identities and controlled write access reduce accidental or malicious modification.","OPERATIONS\n\nAudit administrative changes, monitor permission drift and separate storage management credentials from user access.","LAB\n\nDesign permissions for shared datasets where Team A can read/write, Team B can read-only and administrators retain recovery access.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow would you secure a shared training dataset without preventing 512 nodes from reading it concurrently?", "GOAL\n\nBalance performance, usability and security."),
Day(151,"Filesystem interview checkpoint","Prove distributed filesystem mastery","You should now be able to whiteboard a parallel filesystem, explain metadata/data separation, tune striping, reason about failure and select benchmarks.","WHITEBOARD\n\nClients → metadata → data targets → network → media. Add monitoring and management paths.","QUESTIONS\n\nMetadata storm? Stripe count? Failure group? Recovery throttling? IOR vs FIO? Lustre vs Scale? NFS vs parallel FS? Object vs POSIX?",
"SCENARIO\n\nTraining starts slowly, then accelerates. Identify warm-cache effects, metadata initialization, dataset staging and application behavior before blaming hardware.","LAB\n\nGive yourself 30 minutes to design a 1 PB parallel filesystem for 256 GPUs. Include capacity, bandwidth, metadata, protection and recovery.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain every design decision and what measurement validates it.","STANDARD\n\nYou should sound like an infrastructure engineer who understands distributed filesystems operationally, not someone who memorized product names.")
)
